---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Stores Designer — Andrew Shiau"
  description="A design-system tool at Amazon that helps distributed teams build consistent shopping experiences — the through-line from Luthier."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="back"><a href="/">← Index</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-10"><div class="shrule"></div><h1>Stores Designer</h1></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <p class="dek c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      Helping distributed teams at Amazon build consistent shopping experiences
      without hand-coding each one — the question I carried out of Luthier, at a
      bigger scale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">Frontend engineer — design-system tooling, agent-assisted authoring, and the design system that keeps output consistent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Where</div>
        <div class="ftx">At Amazon · 2023–present.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">The line</div>
        <div class="ftx">The direct successor to <a href="/work/luthier/">Luthier</a>: same premise, larger surface.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid"><div class="rule"></div></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-2"></div>
    <div class="c3-12"><h2>The premise</h2></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <p>
        Many teams, one shopping surface. Left alone, each team rebuilds the same
        components slightly differently and consistency erodes. The job is to make the
        consistent path the easy path — a design system plus tooling that lets people
        build real experiences quickly, on shared rails, without hand-coding every
        screen.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's the same reframe as Luthier — the tool authors against a system so the
        output is correct by construction — applied to interactive shopping
        experiences instead of marketing graphics, and to many teams instead of one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid"><div class="rule"></div></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-2"></div>
    <div class="c3-12"><h2>What I work on</h2></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <p>
        Front-end for the authoring tool, the plumbing that keeps generated output on
        the design system, and the reliability of the loop when an agent is doing the
        building. Much of the AI work on this site — verification loops, keeping a
        human in the judgment seat — comes straight from this problem.
      </p>
      <p class="c1-8" style="color:var(--faint);font-size:14px">
        This is production work at Amazon, so it's described here at a high level —
        no internal detail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
